Crash dumping using Kdump on the sh7372 Mackerel board

Kdump is a well known crash dumping solution coming from the
enterprise world. The same solution is also available for embedded
systems such as the sh7372 Mackerel board. This small document gives
an example how to use Kdump on an embedded board.

What is Kdump?

Kdump allows you to save an image of a crashed system to later let you
to analyze it using a tool called crash. Exactly how and where you
save the crash image is application specific. In this example the
crash image will be saved to the NOR Flash mounted on the sh7372
Mackerel board and/or transferred via Ethernet to the host.

- - -

Kdump usage overview:

1) Start of primary kernel on target
2) Load of secondary kernel via kexec
3) Trigger crash
4) Start of secondary kernel on target
5) Saving of crash image
6) Transfer of crash image to host machine
7) crash image analysis on host via crash tool

- - -

Usage details:

1) Start of primary kernel on target

Boot the uImage file built in the step "1) Primary kernel
configuration and build" below.

2) Load of secondary kernel via kexec

Use kexec built in the step "3) Building kexec-tools" below. Use the
uImage-crash file built in step "2) Secondary kernel configuration and
build" below. Load the secondary kernel using the following command:
# kexec -p /uImage-crash --append="console=ttySC0,115200 mem=32M@0x42000000"
Please note that the mem= parameter above needs to match the
crashkernel= kernel command line parameter compiled-in the primary
kernel.

3) Trigger crash

Wait for a bug to hit or use following to force a trigger:
# echo c > /proc/sysrq-trigger

4) Start of secondary kernel on target

No need to do anything - this happens automatically when a crash is triggered

5) Saving the crash image

Inside the crash kernel the file /proc/vmcore is representing the
crash image. On the sh7372 Mackerel board, use the following to save
the crash image to NOR Flash:
# dd if=/proc/vmcore of=/dev/mtdblock4
The /proc/vmcore file contains all memory used by primary kernel
except the area reserved for the crash kernel. eMMC, NAND flash or SD
cards may be faster compared to NOR Flash but they also come with a
set of restrictions that are out of scope for this example. Another
solution for development is to directly transfer via network:
# ifconfig eth0 192.168.99.5 up
# cat /proc/vmcore | tftp -l - -r mackerel/vmcore -p -b 1500 192.168.99.1

6) Transfer of crash image to host machine

After saving the crash image reboot the board and copy the image from
/dev/mtdblock4 to your host machine as "vmcore". This step can be
omitted if the image already has been transferred in 5) above.

7) crash image analysis on host via crash tool

This step requires the crash tool built in "4) Building crash". The
crash tool also needs the crash image transferred in "6) Transfer of
crash image to host machine" and a vmlinux file from "1) Primary
kernel configuration and build". To start the interactive crash tool
use the following command:
$ crash vmlinux vmcore
...
crash>
The "sys" command can be used to extract information like this:
$ echo sys | crash -s vmlinux-mackerel vmcore
WARNING: cannot determine how modules are linked
WARNING: no kernel module access

      KERNEL: /home/damm/vmlinux-mackerel
    DUMPFILE: /home/damm/vmcore-mackerel
        CPUS: 1
        DATE: Thu Jan  1 09:00:10 1970
      UPTIME: 00:00:10
LOAD AVERAGE: 0.00, 0.00, 0.00
       TASKS: 15
    NODENAME: (none)
     RELEASE: 3.0.0-rc3
     VERSION: #2 Mon Jun 20 21:46:48 JST 2011
     MACHINE: armv7l  (unknown Mhz)
      MEMORY: 96 MB
       PANIC: "Internal error: Oops: 817 [#1]" (check log for details)


For more information have a look at the crash website and/or at the
README file shipped with the source code of the crash tool.

- - -

Software setup overview:

1) Primary kernel configuration and build
2) Secondary kernel configuration and build
3) Building kexec-tools
4) Building crash

- - -

Software setup details:


1) Primary kernel configuration and build

The primary kernel is the main kernel running on your embedded system.
In the case of the sh7372 Mackerel board, the kernel configuration
file is the attached
"linux-3.0-rc-arm-shmobile-mackerel-crash-1st-20110620.config". The
most important points of this configuration are:

A) CONFIG_KEXEC=y
Enables the kexec system call, together with kdump primary kernel support.

B) CONFIG_ATAGS_PROC=y
ARM-specific option needed to pass boot loader tags from primary
kernel to secondary.

C) CONFIG_CMDLINE_FORCE=y
Use the compiled-in kernel command line regardsless of what the boot
loader provides.

D) CONFIG_CMDLINE="console=ttySC0,115200 earlyprintk=sh-sci.0,115200
ignore_loglevel mem=96M@0x40000000 crashkernel=32M@0x42000000"

D.1) console=, earlyprintk=, ignore_loglevel
These point out the serial console and makes sure all messages are printed

D.2) mem=96M@0x40000000
Limit the amount of primary kernel memory from 256MB to 96MB to reduce
the size of the crash image. Without this step the vmcore file will
not fit into the 128 MiB NOR flash.

D.3) crashkernel=32M@0x42000000
The secondary kernel should run in a 32MiB window at 0x42000000.

E) CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO=y
Debugging needs to be enabled to make crash work

F) CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_REDUCED=n
Reduced debugging needs to be disabled.

Linux-3.0-rc git 95d17b7168eacf1f97f2d1d087ebe7a648c30051 is known to
be working. Build the kernel as a regular uImage and use this uImage
with u-boot. The file vmlinux is needed by the crash tool, so save
that for later.


2) Secondary kernel configuration and build

The secondary kernel is the crash kernel running on your embedded
system. In the case of the sh7372 Mackerel board, the kernel
configuration file is the attached
"linux-3.0-rc-arm-shmobile-mackerel-crash-2nd-20110620.config". The
most important points of this configuration are:

A) CONFIG_CRASH_DUMP=y
Enable the kdump feature. Used when building a crash kernel.

B) CONFIG_PROC_VMCORE=y
Enable the /proc/vmcore parser.

C) CONFIG_MEMORY_START=0x42000000
This must match the crashkernel= primary kernel command line option

D) CONFIG_MEMORY_SIZE=0x02000000
This must match the crashkernel= primary kernel command line option

E) CONFIG_CMDLINE_FORCE=n
Use the kernel command line provided by kexec-tools.

Linux-3.0-rc or more exactly linux-2.6 git
95d17b7168eacf1f97f2d1d087ebe7a648c30051 is known to be working, but
the attached prototype patch
"linux-3.0-arm-crash-copy-oldmem-page-ioremap-fix-20110618.patch" is
needed to properly access the /proc/vmcore file. Build the kernel as a
regular uImage. This image is referred to as uImage-crash.


3) Building kexec-tools

kexec-tools contains the user space program "kexec" which is used to
load the secondary kernel into memory from the primary kernel. Known
working version is 9ec3fac7e8840fb31891ba49a626c5dd33e09e86 from
"git://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/utils/kernel/kexec/kexec-tools.git". Use
the following steps to cross compile:

A) ./bootstrap
B) CC=_gcc LDFLAGS=-static ./configure --host=arm-none-linux-gnueabi
Replace _gcc with your ARM cross compiler. Remove the LDFLAGS bit if
you want a dynamic binary.
C) make
D) file build/sbin/kexec
This is the kexec binary that you will use to load the secondary kernel.


4) Building crash

Unpack crash-5.1.6.tar.gz and start the build by using:
$ echo ARM > gdb-7.0/crash.target
$ make target=ARM
...
$ file ./crash
This is the crash binary that you will use to analyze the vmcore file.

